.TH DIDL 5 2026-10-30 DarqOS "DarqOS File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
didl \- DarqOS interface definition language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB name .didl
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Darq Interface Definition Language (DIDL) describes the messages
DarqOS processes exchange over ports: the requests a service accepts,
the responses it sends back, and the enums and structs they're made
of.
.PP
A DIDL file is compiled by
.BR didlc (1)
into an intermediate representation (IR), which gives the exact byte
layout of every message, and from which code generators produce
what programs use to build and read them:
.BR didlpygen (1)
for Python, and
.BR didlcgen (1)
for C.
.PP
A file defines one
.IR library :
a set of
.IR enums ,
.I structs
and
.IR protocols .
A protocol is a set of
.IR methods ,
each a request, with or without a response.
For example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
// An example: a file browser's protocol.

library example.files;

enum Status : uint32 {
    OK = 0;
    NOT_FOUND = 1;
    NOT_ALLOWED = 0x10;
}

// A directory entry.
struct Entry {
    string name;
    uint64 size;
    bool directory;
}

protocol Files {
    // List a directory, a page at a time.
    1: List(string path, uint32 max) \-> (sequence<Entry> entries, Status status);

    // Open a file: its port is returned.
    2: Open(string path) \-> (port file, Status status);

    // Forget a path's cached entries.  One\-way.
    3: Forget(string path);
}
.fi
.RE
.SH LEXICAL STRUCTURE
A DIDL file is ASCII text.
Other characters may appear only in comments.
.SS Whitespace and comments
Spaces, tabs and newlines separate tokens, and are otherwise
ignored: a declaration may span lines, or several share one.
.PP
A comment starts with
.B //
and runs to the end of the line.
There are no block comments:
.B /*
is an error.
.SS Identifiers
An identifier is a letter or underscore, followed by any number of
letters, digits, underscores and dots:
.PP
.RS
.B [a\-zA\-Z_][a\-zA\-Z0\-9_.]*
.RE
.PP
Identifiers are case-sensitive.
Dots are for library names, such as
.BR darq.storage :
no other name may contain them (see
.BR NAMES ).
.SS Keywords
.BR enum ,
.BR library ,
.B protocol
and
.B struct
are reserved: they can't be used as identifiers, even as field names.
The type names, such as
.B uint32
and
.BR sequence ,
are not keywords, but can't be used as the names of enums or structs.
.SS Numbers
A number is decimal, or hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
or
.B 0X
prefix, and may be negative, with a
.B \-
directly before it:
.BR 42 ,
.BR 0x2A ,
.BR \-1 .
Leading zeros don't make a number octal:
.B 010
is ten.
Numbers are used for enum member values and method numbers.
.SS Punctuation
.TS
l l.
\fB{ }\fP	enclose the body of an enum, struct or protocol
\fB( )\fP	enclose a method's request or response fields
\fB< >\fP	enclose a sequence's element type
\fB;\fP	ends a library declaration, an enum member, a field, or a method
\fB,\fP	separates a method's fields
\fB:\fP	follows a method number, and introduces an enum's base type
\fB=\fP	gives an enum member its value
\fB\->\fP	introduces a method's response
.TE
.SH GRAMMAR
In this grammar,
.I italic
names are rules, bold text is literal,
.RI [ x ]
is optional, and
.IR x ...
is zero or more of
.IR x .
.PP
.RS
.nf
.IR file "        = " declaration ...
.IR declaration " = " library " | " enum " | " struct " | " protocol
.IR library "     = " \fBlibrary\fP " " identifier " " \fB;\fP
.IR enum "        = " \fBenum\fP " " identifier " " \fB:\fP " " identifier " " \fB{\fP " " member "... " \fB}\fP
.IR member "      = " identifier " " \fB=\fP " " number " " \fB;\fP
.IR struct "      = " \fBstruct\fP " " identifier " " \fB{\fP " " field "... " \fB}\fP
.IR field "       = " type " " identifier " " \fB;\fP
.IR protocol "    = " \fBprotocol\fP " " identifier " " \fB{\fP " " method "... " \fB}\fP
.IR method "      = " number " " \fB:\fP " " identifier " " \fB(\fP " " fields " " \fB)\fP " [ " \fB\->\fP " " \fB(\fP " " fields " " \fB)\fP " ] " \fB;\fP
.IR fields "      = [ " type " " identifier " [ " \fB,\fP " " type " " identifier " ]... ]"
.IR type "        = " identifier " | " \fBsequence\fP " " \fB<\fP " " type " " \fB>\fP
.fi
.RE
.PP
A field list in a method has no trailing comma.
No semicolon follows the closing brace of an enum, struct or protocol.
.SH DECLARATIONS
Declarations may appear in any order: a type may be used before it's
declared.
.SS Library
.PP
.RS
.B library darq.storage;
.RE
.PP
Names the library, which is recorded in the IR and the generated
module; without
.BR \-o ,
.BR didlpygen (1)
names its output after it.
Each file has exactly one library declaration, which by convention
comes before its other declarations; DarqOS's are named after their
service, as
.BR darq.history .
Its name is the only one that may contain dots.
.SS Enums
.PP
.RS
.nf
enum Status : uint32 {
    OK = 0;
    NOT_FOUND = 1;
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
An enum names a set of integer values.
Its base type, after the colon, sets its size and alignment in a
message, and the range of its values.
It must be an integer type:
.BR int8 ,
.BR uint8 ,
.BR int16 ,
.BR uint16 ,
.BR int32 ,
.BR uint32 ,
.B int64
or
.BR uint64 ;
not
.B bool
or a real type.
.PP
An enum has at least one member.
Each member's value is given explicitly, must fit the base type, and
must be unique within the enum, as must each member's name.
.PP
An enum field holds one of its members: decoding a value that isn't
one fails.
So an enum isn't a set of flags; a field holding a combination of
bits should be an integer, with an enum naming the bits, as
.I lib/didl/compositor.didl
does for its
.BR Modifier s.
.PP
Where an enum has a member with value 0, a new message's field of that
type starts with it, otherwise with its first member; by convention,
a
.B Status
enum's
.B OK
is 0.
.SS Structs
.PP
.RS
.nf
struct Entry {
    string name;
    uint64 size;
    bool directory;
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A struct is a sequence of named fields, each of any type, including
other structs, enums and sequences.
A struct may be empty, but then can't be a sequence's element type.
.PP
A struct may not contain itself, directly, or through other structs,
or in a sequence: there are no recursive types.
.SS Protocols
.PP
.RS
.nf
protocol Files {
    1: List(string path, uint32 max) \-> (sequence<Entry> entries, Status status);
    3: Forget(string path);
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
A protocol is a set of methods.
Each method has a number, a name, and a request: its fields, in
parentheses, which may be empty.
.PP
A method with
.B \->
and a second field list has a response: a client sends the request,
and waits for the response.
A method without one is
.IR one-way :
it's just sent, and has no reply.
A response may have no fields,
.BR "\-> ()" ,
when the client only needs to know the request was handled.
.PP
Each method's number identifies it in the message header.
Numbers run from 1 to 65535, must be unique within the protocol, and
are assigned by hand: they needn't be in order, or contiguous.
A method's number should never be changed, or reused for a different
method, once programs use it; a new method gets a new number.
.PP
A protocol may have no methods.
One library may have several protocols, such as a service's own and
the one spoken on its bootstrap port.
.SH TYPES
.TS
l l l l.
\fBType\fP	\fBSize\fP	\fBAlign\fP	\fBHolds\fP
bool	1	1	true (1) or false (0)
int8, uint8	1	1	8-bit integer, signed or not
int16, uint16	2	2	16-bit integer
int32, uint32	4	4	32-bit integer
int64, uint64	8	8	64-bit integer
real32	4	4	IEEE 754 single precision
real64	8	8	IEEE 754 double precision
opaque	8	4	a sequence of bytes, of any length
string	8	4	UTF-8 text, of any length
sequence<\fIT\fP>	8	4	elements of type \fIT\fP, any number
port	2	2	a port, transferred with the message
\fIenum\fP	base	base	one of its members
\fIstruct\fP	layout	layout	its fields
.TE
.PP
An enum's size and alignment are its base type's; a struct's come from
its layout (see
.BR LAYOUT ).
Integers and reals are little-endian.
.TP
.BR opaque ", " string
In the message, a descriptor: a
.B uint32
offset of the value from the start of the message, then its
.B uint32
length.
The bytes themselves follow the message's fixed part (see
.BR "WIRE FORMAT" ).
A string's bytes must be valid UTF-8, with no NUL, and are always
followed by a NUL, which isn't counted in its length, so it can be
used in place as a C string.
.TP
.BI sequence< T >
In the message, a descriptor: a
.B uint32
offset of an array of the elements, then their
.B uint32
count.
The array follows the message's fixed part, holding each element at a
stride of
.IR T 's
size.
.I T
may be any type, including another sequence, such as
.BR sequence<sequence<string>> ,
but not an empty struct.
.TP
.B port
A
.B uint16
index into the list of handles transferred with the message, or
0xFFFF for no port.
The p-kernel translates each handle into one the receiver holds, so a
port field is how a send right to a port is passed to another process
(see
.IR doc/ipc.rst ).
A message transfers at most 64 handles.
.SH NAMES
Names are case-sensitive, and each kind has its own rules.
.B didlc
enforces the language's rules, below, and
.BR didlpygen (1)
those for Python; the others are conventions.
No name but the library's may contain dots.
.TP
.IR Types " and " protocols
Enum, struct and protocol names share one namespace: no two of them,
of whichever kind, can have the same name.
Nor can any have the name of a built-in type, such as
.B string
or
.BR sequence .
By convention, they're in CamelCase.
.TP
.I Methods
A method's name must be unique within its protocol.
By convention, methods are in CamelCase:
.B didlpygen
makes each a Python method in snake case
.RB ( GetEvents
becomes
.BR get_events ),
and those must be distinct too, and not names its base classes use,
such as
.B call
or
.BR dispatch .
.IP
A method's messages are named after it: a one-way method's message has
its name, and a method with a response has
.IB name Request
and
.IB name Response\fR.
Those names must be distinct from each other, and from the types and
protocols, across the whole library: two protocols in one library
can't have methods with the same name.
.TP
.I Fields
A field's name must be unique within its struct, or within its
method's request or response.
A request's or response's field must not be named
.BR txid ,
which every message has, in its header.
By convention, fields are in snake case.
.TP
.I Enum members
A member's name must be unique within its enum, and not start with an
underscore.
By convention, members are in upper case.
.PP
Every name but the library's must also be a valid identifier, and not
a keyword, in the languages code is generated for: for Python, not a
name like
.BR class ,
.B None
or
.BR lambda .
.B didlpygen
checks that.
.SH LAYOUT
Each message's body, like each struct, is laid out in field order,
following the C ABI: each field at the next offset aligned to its
type's alignment, the whole aligned to its most strictly aligned
field, and padded to a multiple of that.
So reordering fields changes the layout, and wastes less padding with
the most strictly aligned first.
.PP
In the example above,
.B Entry
is 24 bytes, aligned to 8:
.B name
at offset 0 (8 bytes),
.B size
at 8 (8 bytes),
.B directory
at 16 (1 byte), and 7 bytes of padding.
.BR Open 's
response is 8 bytes:
.B file
at 0 (2 bytes), 2 bytes of padding, and
.B status
at 4 (4 bytes).
.PP
Every message starts with an 8-byte header: a
.B uint32
transaction identifier
.RB ( txid ),
0 for a one-way message; a
.B uint16
of flags; and the
.B uint16
method number.
The body follows it, so
.BR Open 's
response is 16 bytes, with no out-of-line values.
See
.BR didlc (1)
for the header.
.SH WIRE FORMAT
A message is used in place, where it's built or received, by any
language, and could be shared between processes without copying: so
nothing in it is an address, and each descriptor is an offset from the
start of the message.
.IP \(bu 3
A message starts on an 8-byte boundary.
Its fields are little-endian, at their offsets.
.IP \(bu
The values of its opaques, strings and sequences (an array of the
elements, at a stride of the element type's size) follow its fixed
part.
Each starts on an 8-byte boundary, and is padded with zeros to a
multiple of 8 bytes: a string's value includes its NUL.
.IP \(bu
The values may be in any order, so a message can be built with its
fields set in any order, but no two may overlap: a value can't be
referred to twice.
.IP \(bu
A
.B bool
is 0 or 1, an enum's value is one of its members, and a port's index
is within the handles transferred with the message, or 0xFFFF.
.PP
A reader checks all of this, before it relies on any of it: a message
that breaks a rule is rejected.
.SH CONVENTIONS
DarqOS's own DIDL files follow these conventions, beyond the naming
ones:
.IP \(bu 3
A header comment says what the protocols are for, and refers to the
document describing them.
.IP \(bu
A service's errors are a
.B Status
enum, based on
.BR uint32 ,
with
.B OK
as 0, returned as the last field of each response that can fail.
An error reply (see
.BR darq.dipc.ErrorReply )
is for a request that couldn't be handled at all, not for a request
that failed for a reason the protocol defines.
.IP \(bu
Each protocol, method, and non-obvious field or member has a comment
before it: what it does, who sends it, and what it returns, and for a
service, the name it's registered as.
.IP \(bu
A method that may take a long time to answer says so: the service
defers its reply.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /darq/lib/didl/*.didl
DarqOS's DIDL files, installed.
.TP
.I lib/didl/*.didl
The same, in the source tree.
.TP
.I darq/protocols/*.py
The Python modules generated from them, by
.BR "make protocols" ,
which runs
.BR didlc (1)
and
.BR didlpygen (1)
for each.
The modules are committed with the DIDL files: run it after changing
one.
.TP
.IR /darq/lib/c/darq/dipc.h ", " /darq/lib/c/darq/dipc.c
The C runtime for code generated by
.BR didlcgen (1),
.I lib/c/darq
in the source tree.
C is generated when a program that uses it is built.
.SH BUGS
There are no constants, no optional fields, and no versioning: a
protocol changes compatibly only by adding methods.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR didlc (1),
.BR didlcgen (1),
.BR didlpygen (1),
DarqOS documentation,
.IR doc/ipc.rst .
